.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:7:**Depends on:** [ADR-0104](0104-execution-mode-and-mode-aware-services.md) (prerequisite for mode enforcement in §3, audit in §8, transactions in §2)  
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:21:- Run on a schedule or in response to system events, not user actions.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:41:Every ability in the Maintenance category operates under all rules of [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) plus the eleven constraints below, covering idempotency, transactional boundaries, execution-mode and dry-run semantics, multi-dimensional change budgets, signal containment, actor policy, blast radius with user-confirmed global, operational audit records, snapshot-diff evaluation, scoped observability, and compile-time separation. These constraints are enforced by type system, registry metadata, runtime checks, and review discipline — not by convention.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:52:**Exception:** An ability that explicitly accumulates (e.g., `archive_old_signals`) declares `idempotent = false` in its registry entry and must document what it accumulates and why. These are audited manually.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:109:| Audit records | Written to `maintenance_audit` | Captured to tracer only | Captured to tracer only (normalized) | Written to `maintenance_audit` |
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:117:- **Audit contract resolution.** `MaintenanceIntent` and `MaintenanceOutcome` (§8) are operational audit records, not signals. In `Live` mode, they are persisted to a dedicated `maintenance_audit` table, not the signal bus. In `Simulate` and `Evaluate` modes, they are captured to the tracer. `Live + dry_run=true` writes them to the audit table — the invocation happened; the mutations were chosen to be skipped.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:195:**Why this structure.** `data_source` remains the truthful origin of the data (for reliability weighting, purge cascades, trust). Maintenance context is orthogonal metadata, not a conflicting `source`. A signal emitted by a maintenance job that updates health scoring still has `data_source = ai` (or whatever computed it); its maintenance-invocation-id tells the audit story without rewriting its data lineage.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:249:Every maintenance ability writes two audit records — NOT signals (Codex finding #16). Signals feed intelligence fusion, health scoring, and propagation; they affect the product's understanding of the world. Maintenance audit records are operational metadata describing what the system did to itself. Mixing them would contaminate scoring and propagation.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:251:Audit records are written to a dedicated `maintenance_audit` table:
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:290:`affected_entity_ids` stores the list of entity IDs the invocation planned to touch (Intent) or did touch (Outcome). It is stored as a JSON array column and indexed via a supporting `maintenance_audit_entity_xref` table that maps `(invocation_id, entity_id)` — SQLite does not support GIN-style array indexes, so cross-reference via a join table is the idiomatic pattern.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:303:- **Live and Live+dry_run:** records persist to `maintenance_audit`.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:307:- The registry's maintenance-ability wrapper writes audit records automatically. Individual ability implementations cannot skip them.
.docs/decisions/0103-maintenance-ability-safety-constraints.md:308:- Audit writes go through a dedicated `audit` service that is not subject to the maintenance ability's change budget (audit must not be rationed).
