   173	- `find_corroborating_evidence` Read ability — v1.4.1; v1.4.0 ships the queue + skeleton.
   174	
   175	## Consequences
   176	
   177	### Positive
   178	
   179	- Trust Compiler's `user_feedback_weight` factor has a typed input, not a placeholder.
   180	- Per-action behavior is specified before code lands. No ambiguity about whether `MarkFalse` punishes the source or the claim.

 succeeded in 477ms:
    20	### 1. Immutable assertion core
    21	
    22	Once a claim is written, its `text`, `claim_type`, `subject_ref`, `source_asof`, and `created_at` are immutable. No UPDATE of those columns from anywhere in the codebase except migration paths.
    23	
    24	Derived columns (`trust_score`, `trust_computed_at`, `trust_version`, `claim_state`, `surfacing_state`, `demotion_reason`, `reactivated_at`, `superseded_by`) are allowed to mutate, routed exclusively through `services/claims.rs`.
    25	
    26	Enforcement: CI lint allowlist per column (DOS-7 amendment D).
    27	
    28	### 2. Additive weight updates
    29	
    30	Corroboration strength accumulates via `UPDATE claim_corroborations SET strength = ?, reinforcement_count = ?, last_reinforced_at = ?` — the one exception to "append-only" in the corroboration layer, allowed because the underlying fact (same source corroborating the same claim again) is logically a reinforcement of an existing edge, not a new fact.
    31	
    32	All other weight/trust changes produce new rows (signal events, feedback rows, trust recompute events), never destructive rewrites of the previous state.
    33	
    34	Enforcement: write-path restricted to `services/claims.rs::record_corroboration`; all other UPDATEs on `claim_corroborations` rejected by CI lint.
    35	
    36	### 3. Dormancy is recoverable, deletion is not
    37	
    38	`DELETE FROM intelligence_claims` is forbidden outside user-data-deletion flows (GDPR/CCPA). Same for `claim_corroborations` and `claim_contradictions`.
    39	
    40	Stale or unreinforced claims transition to `surfacing_state='dormant'`. Dormant claims stay in storage, queryable on opt-in, reactivatable on new evidence. The lifecycle is two-column (`claim_state` = user intent; `surfacing_state` = system rendering) to prevent ghost-resurrection bugs that come from conflating the two.
    41	
    42	Enforcement: CI lint grep for `DELETE FROM intelligence_claims|claim_corroborations|claim_contradictions` outside the allowed paths.
    43	
    44	### 4. Retrieval is additive, not distortive
    45	
    46	Passive user engagement (view, dwell, skip) produces events that affect ranking and surfacing. It does not modify trust, does not rewrite claim text, does not alter provenance.
    47	
    48	Explicit user judgment (correction, tombstone, confirmation) flows through `claim_feedback` per ADR-0123 typed feedback semantics. Explicit judgment affects trust via DOS-5's `fb` factor.
    49	
    50	The two are not the same thing and never get conflated at the storage layer. Inflating "what is true" based on "what the user paid attention to" is a bug, not a feature.
    51	
    52	### 5. Schema-based compression is allowed, anomaly suppression is not
    53	
    54	When a new signal fits an existing claim schema (ADR-0125 claim type registry), it assimilates: strengthens corroborations, bumps freshness, does not produce a new surface notification.
    55	
    56	When a new signal does NOT fit the schema (missing required claim, stale past typical freshness, unexpected presence, cadence break, out-of-range value), it surfaces as a deviation. Deviations are the foreground of the briefing, not the background.
    57	
    58	Invariant: the schema assimilation pass is allowed to lower the weight of routine background claims. It is NOT allowed to lower the weight of any claim the deviation detector has flagged. Structural, not best-effort.
    59	
    60	Enforcement: precedence check in the consolidation + trust compiler aggregation paths; unit test asserts deviation-flagged claims never lose weight via routine compression.
    61	
    62	### 6. Contradiction is a fork, not a winner-picking
    63	
    64	When a new signal directly contradicts an existing claim (same subject + claim_type, incompatible value, existing aggregate strength ≥ 0.4), the substrate creates a `claim_contradictions` row with `branch_kind='contradiction'`. Both claims stay active and queryable. Neither is authoritative for default display until reconciled.
    65	
    66	Reconciliation happens via one of four explicit kinds (`user_picked_winner`, `evidence_converged`, `merged_as_qualified`, `both_dormant`). Auto-supersession for time-varying claim types (renewal_date, contract_end, ARR) records `reconciliation_kind='evidence_converged'` and uses ADR-0113's supersede path — no new supersession mechanism.
    67	
    68	The brain picks a winner and forgets the loser. We fork and keep both lines, because we can.
    69	
    70	### 7. Diversity of sources beats volume
    71	
    72	Ten Gong transcripts of the same meeting are not ten sources. Three independent sources (Salesforce + Zendesk + Gong) asserting the same thing is stronger evidence than a hundred same-source repetitions. The corroboration math reflects this: each `(claim_id, data_source)` edge accumulates strength with diminishing returns, and claim-level confidence aggregates via noisy-OR over independent edges.
    73	
    74	Same-source reinforcement: `strength += log(n+2)/log(n+1)` — approaches 1.0 asymptotically.
    75	Independent-source aggregate: `1 - product(1 - strength_i)` — combines across distinct sources.
    76	
    77	Enforced by DOS-7 amendment A and DOS-5 corr-factor amendment.
    78	
    79	### 8. Homeostatic budgets by category, not by entity
    80	
    81	An entity-wide scalar "attention budget" that demotes the lowest-weight claim when exceeded is wrong: it buries rare-but-important claims (first signal of a deviation) under common-but-routine ones (weekly status updates).
    82	
    83	Budgets, if used, are per claim_type + sensitivity + surface. A critical-sensitivity health claim does not compete for the same attention slot as a routine adoption-metric. The claim type registry (ADR-0125) carries the per-type budget policy, not a global constant.
    84	
    85	### 9. Engagement affects ranking; feedback affects trust
    86	
    87	Two separate streams, two separate tables, never fused.
    88	
    89	- Engagement events (view, dwell, expand, scroll-past, soft-dismiss) → `claim_engagement_events` → ranking input for the surfacing layer.
    90	- Feedback events (correction, confirmation, tombstone) → `claim_feedback` (ADR-0123 typed) → DOS-5's `fb` factor → trust.
    91	
    92	A claim the user ignores stays exactly as true as the evidence says it is. A claim the user corrects becomes a different claim, with the correction as a new source.
    93	
    94	### 10. Substrate vocabulary is canonical
    95	
    96	Drift in terminology across tickets is drift in implementation. The substrate uses a fixed vocabulary:
    97	
    98	- **claim** — immutable assertion
    99	- **corroboration** — evidence row supporting a claim, with strength
   100	- **contradiction** — divergent claim on same subject + type, with reconciliation state
   101	- **source** — a DataSource variant (DOS-212) with `source_asof`
   102	- **edge** — claim-derived relationship in the semantic graph (DOS-265), distinct from corroboration evidence
   103	- **signal** — runtime event per ADR-0115 emitted on substrate writes
   104	- **engagement** — passive telemetry, ranking-only
   105	- **feedback** — explicit user judgment, trust-affecting

 succeeded in 476ms:
   103	### 5. Tombstones
   104	
   105	A tombstone is a claim with:
   106	
   107	- `actor = 'user'` (or `user_removal` for the specific tombstone variant when the user explicitly cleared a field)
   108	- `claim_text = NULL`
   109	- `retraction_reason = 'user_removal'`
   110	- `claim_state = 'committed'` (tombstones commit immediately per §3)
   111	
   112	Tombstones participate in supersede semantics: when a user removes a field value, a tombstone claim is written and supersedes any prior committed claim at the same `(entity_id, claim_type, field_path)`.
   113	
   114	**Writers must consult tombstones before proposing — PRE-GATE CHECK (clarified 2026-04-20 per outside voice finding #3).** The tombstone check is **not** part of the commit policy gate. It is a **hard pre-gate rejection** that runs inside `services::claims::propose_claim` before any commit policy evaluation. Order of operations:
   115	
   116	1. Tombstone check (this §5) — reject any actor targeting a tombstoned `field_path` within the tombstone window, regardless of commit policy config.
   117	2. Dedup check (§8) — match existing claim; increment corroboration if yes.
   118	3. Commit policy gate (§3) — if actor is `agent` and policy is `gated`, run trust + corroboration logic; otherwise `immediate`.
   119	
   120	This order matters because it means **the ghost-resurrection fix is live in v1.4.0 even when the commit policy config is `immediate` for all actor classes** (which R1.9 specifies for v1.4.0). The tombstone protection does not depend on the gate activating; it's a structural pre-check. When v1.4.1 flips agent policy to `gated`, the tombstone check still runs first.
   121	
   122	An agent wishing to override a tombstone must produce a higher-threshold corroboration (e.g., three independent sources within 7 days) and surface the override as a contradiction (§7) rather than a silent commit.
   123	
   124	This closes the ghost-resurrection bug documented in [ADR-0118](0118-dailyos-as-ai-harness-principles-and-residual-gaps.md) Gap D **in v1.4.0**, not deferred to v1.4.1.
   125	
   131	CREATE TABLE agent_trust_ledger (
   132	  agent_name        TEXT NOT NULL,
   133	  agent_version     TEXT NOT NULL,
   134	  claim_type        TEXT NOT NULL,
   135	  alpha             REAL NOT NULL DEFAULT 1.0,   -- corroborated / correct
   136	  beta              REAL NOT NULL DEFAULT 1.0,   -- contradicted / incorrect
   137	  posterior_score   REAL NOT NULL DEFAULT 0.5,   -- Beta posterior mean, cached
   138	  last_updated      TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,
   139	  PRIMARY KEY (agent_name, agent_version, claim_type)
   140	);
   141	```
   142	
   143	Updates:
   144	
   145	- User accepts an agent claim via corroboration or explicit confirmation → `alpha += 1`.
   146	- User contradicts an agent claim (retraction, correction, "wrong") → `beta += 1`.
   147	- Corroboration by an independent source within the window → `alpha += 0.5`.
   148	- Contradiction by an independent source → `beta += 0.5`.
   149	
   150	`posterior_score` is the Beta(α, β) mean, recomputed on update. Agents with `posterior_score` below a configurable floor (default 0.45) have all claims auto-routed to the Analysis Inbox regardless of trust-and-corroboration gate outcome, until their score recovers.

